WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.590
Foreign Influence in Canadian Democracy.

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:06.897
Documented foreign interference in Canadian elections and institutions.

00:00:06.897 --> 00:00:14.193
The Hogue Commission findings, NSICOP intelligence reports, and the institutional response to confirmed threats against democratic sovereignty.

00:00:14.193 --> 00:00:21.397
Two election cycles affected, 11 ridings targeted, 3 state actors identified, zero prosecutions launched.

00:00:21.397 --> 00:00:27.272
The Hogue Commission examined foreign interference in the 2019 and 2021 federal elections.

00:00:27.272 --> 00:00:34.193
Key findings: foreign interference occurred in both elections and may have affected results in at least 11 ridings.

00:00:34.193 --> 00:00:39.579
Intelligence about interference reached senior government officials but the response was insufficient.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.420
Canada lacks a mechanism to act on interference intelligence during election periods.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:01:03.534
Documented foreign interference methods include: nomination manipulation through busing supporters, indirect campaign financing through intermediaries, coercion of diaspora communities, state-affiliated information operations and social media campaigns, elite capture of elected officials, and institutional lobbying both registered and unregistered.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:04.977
The Accountability Gap.

00:01:04.977 --> 00:01:17.943
Despite two public inquiries, an NSICOP report identifying MPs wittingly assisting foreign states, confirmed CSIS intelligence, and a legislative response that arrived years late — zero prosecutions have been launched.

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:22.420
No MP identified in the NSICOP report has been publicly named or charged.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:27.272
The foreign agent registry created by Bill C-70 is not retroactive.
